 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nHash generation produces incorrect values for OpenID Connect tokens\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen generating hash values for OpenID Connect ID tokens (like `at_hash` and `c_hash`), the library is producing incorrect hash values that don't match the expected OpenID Connect specification.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom oauthlib.openid.connect.core.grant_types.base import GrantTypeBase\nimport hashlib\n\n# Create a grant type instance\ngrant = GrantTypeBase()\n\n# Test hash generation with a known value\ntest_value = 'Qcb0Orv1zh30vL1MPRsbm-diHiMwcLyZvn1arpZv-Jxf_11jnpEX3Tgfvk'\nresult = grant._hash_token(test_value, hashlib.sha256)\n\nprint(f\"Generated hash: {result}\")\nprint(f\"Expected hash: LDktKdoQak3Pk0cnXxCltA\")\n```\n\nThe generated hash doesn't match what's expected according to the OpenID Connect specification. This affects ID token validation when `at_hash` and `c_hash` claims are present.\n\n#### Expected Behavior\n\nThe hash should be `LDktKdoQak3Pk0cnXxCltA` but the current implementation generates a different value.\n\n#### Actual Behavior\n\nThe hash generation produces an incorrect result, causing ID token validation to fail when these hash claims are verified by relying parties.\n</issue>\n",
